502
00:23:13,479 --> 00:23:15,132
Agent Rigsby

503
00:23:15,219 --> 00:23:18,658
do you consider yourself
an expert on motorcycle gangs?

504
00:23:18,745 --> 00:23:21,095
- I guess.
- No, don't say, "I guess."

505
00:23:21,182 --> 00:23:23,010
Say, "Yes."
You want to project confidence.

506
00:23:23,097 --> 00:23:25,055
Yes, ma'am.

507
00:23:25,142 --> 00:23:26,448
- Aw.
- Mm.

508
00:23:26,535 --> 00:23:28,015
Don't try to distract me.

509
00:23:28,102 --> 00:23:29,582
And does this expertise

510
00:23:29,669 --> 00:23:31,148
come from years
of close contact

511
00:23:31,235 --> 00:23:32,933
with criminal elements
in your own family?

512
00:23:33,020 --> 00:23:34,500
- Hey.
- Don't say, "Hey."

513
00:23:34,587 --> 00:23:37,328
- Just answer the question.
- No. It doesn't.

514
00:23:37,416 --> 00:23:39,374
Isn't it true that
your father, Steven Rigsby

515
00:23:39,461 --> 00:23:42,377
was a member of
the Iron Gods motorcycle gang?

516
00:23:42,464 --> 00:23:43,596
Yes.

517
00:23:43,683 --> 00:23:45,424
So wouldn't it
be fair to say

518
00:23:45,511 --> 00:23:48,252
everything you learned, you know
from your father, the felon?

519
00:23:48,339 --> 00:23:50,472
[sighs]

520
00:23:50,559 --> 00:23:53,257
Babe, I know it's touchy
but if you wait too long--

521
00:23:53,344 --> 00:23:54,607
I think that's enough.
Thanks.

522
00:23:54,694 --> 00:23:57,566
- It's time to get to court.
- Wayne.

523
00:23:57,653 --> 00:23:59,568
Seriously, it was a big help.
Thank you.

524
00:23:59,655 --> 00:24:00,656
[scoffs]

525
00:24:00,743 --> 00:24:02,310
[man clearing throat]

526
00:24:04,486 --> 00:24:05,879
[indistinct chattering]

527
00:24:08,621 --> 00:24:09,970
Arnold Green?

528
00:24:12,799 --> 00:24:14,757
Oh, that's
a great-looking suit

529
00:24:14,844 --> 00:24:17,673
for a policeman.
Most of you have no taste.

530
00:24:17,760 --> 00:24:18,892
I'm not a policeman.

531
00:24:18,979 --> 00:24:20,633
- She is.
- Yes.

532
00:24:20,720 --> 00:24:24,419
I'm Agent Lisbon with the CBI.
This is Patrick Jane.

533
00:24:24,506 --> 00:24:25,986
Would you like a drink?

534
00:24:26,073 --> 00:24:28,597
We're here to talk to you
about Doc Dugan's death.

535
00:24:28,684 --> 00:24:31,382
- We know you know him.
- Oh, this is official. Okay.

536
00:24:31,470 --> 00:24:34,560
Well, my attorney's name is
Don Hendricks. Give him a call.

537
00:24:34,647 --> 00:24:37,258
You wanted Doc to start
buying liquor from you.

538
00:24:37,345 --> 00:24:39,173
I'm a liquor distributor.

539
00:24:39,260 --> 00:24:42,916
Maybe Doc didn't want to.
You didn't like that.

540
00:24:43,003 --> 00:24:45,396
Did you try blackmailing him?
When that didn't work--

541
00:24:45,484 --> 00:24:47,050
Okay, let's just say

542
00:24:47,137 --> 00:24:50,706
that I'm this scary guy
that you imagine me to be.

543
00:24:50,793 --> 00:24:52,491
You think I couldn't
put the fear

544
00:24:52,578 --> 00:24:54,275
'into some
puffed-up pretty boy?'

545
00:24:54,362 --> 00:24:57,060
If I wanted him to buy,
he was going to buy.

546
00:24:57,147 --> 00:24:58,366
Why should I kill him?

547
00:24:58,453 --> 00:25:00,411
He's worth more to me
alive than dead.

548
00:25:00,499 --> 00:25:02,501
Excellent point. Thank you.
Really helpful.

549
00:25:02,588 --> 00:25:04,807
Lisbon, we can go.
We have what we need.

550
00:25:04,894 --> 00:25:06,113
Next time, call my attorney.

551
00:25:06,200 --> 00:25:07,767
Oh, one more thing.

552
00:25:07,854 --> 00:25:10,334
- Arrest this man.
- For what?

553
00:25:10,421 --> 00:25:13,120
- I don't know. For questioning.
- That's not arresting.

554
00:25:13,207 --> 00:25:14,904
Sir, we're gonna call you
for questioning.

555
00:25:14,991 --> 00:25:16,645
I got nothing to say.

556
00:25:16,732 --> 00:25:19,169
I'm sure we can think
of something to talk about.

557
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:25,001
[clicking]

558
00:25:29,789 --> 00:25:31,181
[muttering]

559
00:25:31,268 --> 00:25:33,227
We're getting set up
for Martin. You ready?

560
00:25:33,314 --> 00:25:37,274
Almost. I think I might need
some more CBI stationary.

561
00:25:37,361 --> 00:25:39,320
Yeah. Oh.
That's a good--

562
00:25:39,407 --> 00:25:41,148
Hey, I was drinking that.

563
00:25:41,235 --> 00:25:43,280
Oh, sorry. Works very well.
Look at that.

564
00:25:43,367 --> 00:25:46,762
Aging. Very authentic.
Very authentic.

565
00:25:46,849 --> 00:25:49,156
I love arts and crafts.
It really relaxes me.

566
00:25:49,243 --> 00:25:50,984
Just a little more
of that.

567
00:25:51,071 --> 00:25:52,638
[exhales deeply]

568
00:25:52,725 --> 00:25:56,032
Rigsby. Ah.
Uh, welcome back.

569
00:25:57,468 --> 00:25:58,469
Yes.

570
00:25:58,557 --> 00:26:00,471
Is that Katy Perry
over there?

571
00:26:00,559 --> 00:26:01,603
[camera shutter clicking]

572
00:26:01,690 --> 00:26:03,953
Nice.
Uh, don't smile.

573
00:26:04,040 --> 00:26:05,825
What's the capital
of Idaho?

574
00:26:05,912 --> 00:26:07,435
- Uh..
- Quick, quick, quick.

575
00:26:07,522 --> 00:26:08,784
Uh, Boise?

576
00:26:08,871 --> 00:26:10,656
Bingo.
Time to raise the dead.

577
00:26:10,743 --> 00:26:15,486
Oh. Let that paper dry.
It's very important. Thank you.

578
00:26:15,574 --> 00:26:16,618
What's going on here?

579
00:26:18,141 --> 00:26:19,839
You good cop
or the bad cop?

580
00:26:19,926 --> 00:26:22,058
Oh, I'm not a cop.

581
00:26:22,145 --> 00:26:23,103
I'm a psychic.

582
00:26:23,190 --> 00:26:25,584
Sure you are.

583
00:26:25,671 --> 00:26:28,456
We have Doc Dugan with us.

584
00:26:28,543 --> 00:26:30,763
He's kind of mad at you.

585
00:26:30,850 --> 00:26:31,938
Doc Dugan is here?

586
00:26:32,025 --> 00:26:33,461
Mm-hmm.

587
00:26:33,548 --> 00:26:36,116
Yes, right there
in that chair.

588
00:26:40,250 --> 00:26:42,252
I'm locked in here
with a loon.

589
00:26:42,339 --> 00:26:44,298
Wait till my lawyer hears
about this one.

590
00:26:44,385 --> 00:26:45,778
Nice bracelet.

591
00:26:45,865 --> 00:26:48,607
It's the bullet
that didn't kill you, right?

592
00:26:50,260 --> 00:26:52,436
- How do you know about that?
- Doc told me.

593
00:26:52,523 --> 00:26:54,656
The hell he did.

594
00:26:54,743 --> 00:26:56,049
You're a fruitcake,
aren't you?

595
00:26:56,136 --> 00:26:57,703
Ask him a question.

596
00:27:01,489 --> 00:27:05,145
We had lunch once at this place,
some red tablecloth joint.

597
00:27:05,232 --> 00:27:08,191
What'd I order?

598
00:27:08,278 --> 00:27:10,237
Steak. Bloody.

599
00:27:10,324 --> 00:27:11,630
Yeah, big deal.

600
00:27:11,717 --> 00:27:13,762
Well, it's your question.
Ask a harder one.

601
00:27:16,156 --> 00:27:19,202
[sighs]

602
00:27:19,289 --> 00:27:21,161
[Martin]
'Who was playing
at the restaurant?'

603
00:27:21,248 --> 00:27:22,336
[Doc]
'It was Motown.'

604
00:27:22,423 --> 00:27:24,251
Motown.

605
00:27:24,338 --> 00:27:25,600
Always a great choice.

606
00:27:25,687 --> 00:27:27,297
Or a good guess.

607
00:27:29,473 --> 00:27:32,041
Last time we were together,
what'd I say to him?

608
00:27:33,260 --> 00:27:35,523
Um..

609
00:27:35,610 --> 00:27:36,829
uh, change the subject.

610
00:27:38,178 --> 00:27:39,875
Doc doesn't want
to talk about that.

611
00:27:39,962 --> 00:27:41,268
It unsettles his spirit.

612
00:27:41,355 --> 00:27:43,836
Yeah, I knew
you were full of it.

613
00:27:45,446 --> 00:27:47,448
Alright, let's do it.

614
00:27:47,535 --> 00:27:48,841
He came to see me.

615
00:27:48,928 --> 00:27:51,104
He was wearing
a gray leather jacket

616
00:27:51,191 --> 00:27:54,977
black t-shirt, some kind of
lizard skin cowboy boots.

617
00:27:55,064 --> 00:27:57,240
You wore a gray leather jacket,
a black t-shirt

618
00:27:57,327 --> 00:27:59,721
and lizard skin
cowboy boots.

619
00:27:59,808 --> 00:28:02,115
Crocodile boots, yeah.

620
00:28:02,202 --> 00:28:03,246
How'd you know that?

621
00:28:03,333 --> 00:28:06,075
You spoke
with a gravelly voice

622
00:28:06,162 --> 00:28:10,123
like you had a cold
or a flu or something.

623
00:28:10,210 --> 00:28:13,430
He took out his knife
and he..

624
00:28:13,517 --> 00:28:14,780
...held it against my throat.

625
00:28:14,867 --> 00:28:17,870
You held a knife
to his throat.

626
00:28:17,957 --> 00:28:20,742
He started to cry.

627
00:28:20,829 --> 00:28:22,831
You laughed at him.

628
00:28:22,918 --> 00:28:25,051
Called him a girlie-man.

629
00:28:25,138 --> 00:28:27,444
And.

630
00:28:27,531 --> 00:28:30,360
I said

631
00:28:30,447 --> 00:28:32,188
"Don't cut my face, please."

632
00:28:34,103 --> 00:28:36,410
He said,
"Don't cut my face, please."

633
00:28:38,542 --> 00:28:39,587
He's really here.

634
00:28:39,674 --> 00:28:41,545
Doc. Doc, you here?

635
00:28:41,632 --> 00:28:43,286
[clattering]

636
00:28:43,373 --> 00:28:45,898
Look, I'm sorry, man.
I'm sorry, Doc, okay?

637
00:28:45,985 --> 00:28:47,900
Calm down, Doc.

638
00:28:47,987 --> 00:28:50,859
Tell us everything you know
about Doc, Green, extortion

639
00:28:50,946 --> 00:28:53,383
car bombs. It's the only way
to make it stop.

640
00:28:53,470 --> 00:28:55,342
Just get him out of here, okay?

641
00:28:55,429 --> 00:28:57,518
You know what
you have to do.

642
00:28:57,605 --> 00:28:59,302
I know what to do.
I'll talk!

643
00:28:59,389 --> 00:29:01,478
- Just get him outta here!
- Good.

644
00:29:06,048 --> 00:29:07,833
Martin confessed to extortion,
ratted out his boss as well.

645
00:29:07,920 --> 00:29:10,052
So Green's going down.
Great job, buddy.

646
00:29:10,139 --> 00:29:11,619
But did they kill Jake?

647
00:29:11,706 --> 00:29:14,404
Martin claims he had nothing
to do with the car bomb.

648
00:29:14,491 --> 00:29:16,711
He said there were
some very dangerous men

649
00:29:16,798 --> 00:29:18,147
'at the club Green owns.'

650
00:29:18,234 --> 00:29:21,063
Now one of them
is Louis "Louie Boom" Corrado.

651
00:29:21,150 --> 00:29:23,370
We've got a big file on him.

652
00:29:23,457 --> 00:29:25,502
He's been implicated
in almost a dozen murders

653
00:29:25,589 --> 00:29:27,113
all of them bombings.

654
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:28,201
That sounds promising.

655
00:29:28,288 --> 00:29:29,463
Can I just--

656
00:29:29,550 --> 00:29:31,770
You said you saw
a guy fleeing the scene.

657
00:29:31,857 --> 00:29:33,772
Bring in Louie
for a line-up.

658
00:29:33,859 --> 00:29:35,774
You see his picture,
it taints the ID.

659
00:29:35,861 --> 00:29:37,166
Can I go with you?

660
00:29:37,253 --> 00:29:39,299
Put a SWAT helmet
on me or somethin'?

661
00:29:39,386 --> 00:29:41,214
Sorry. Jane's got
other plans for you.

662
00:29:44,478 --> 00:29:45,609
[door opening]

663
00:29:45,696 --> 00:29:47,611
[telephone ringing in distance]

664
00:30:01,147 --> 00:30:03,236
[groaning]

665
00:30:03,323 --> 00:30:05,847
[container cap clicking]

666
00:30:05,934 --> 00:30:07,544
[pills rattling]

667
00:30:19,818 --> 00:30:22,951
[chuckling]
Does Jane know
you're on his couch?

668
00:30:23,038 --> 00:30:24,257
You hurt your back again?

669
00:30:24,344 --> 00:30:25,562
[sighs]
I'll be fine.

670
00:30:25,649 --> 00:30:26,999
Oh, yeah.
What'd you do?

671
00:30:27,086 --> 00:30:29,610
[sighs]
Tackled a suspect.

672
00:30:29,697 --> 00:30:32,004
You shouldn't tackle people
with you back like that.

673
00:30:32,091 --> 00:30:33,744
[sighs]
Thanks.

674
00:30:33,832 --> 00:30:35,268
We're gonna go
raid the vespers.

675
00:30:35,355 --> 00:30:37,531
Lisbon says to stay here,
man the phones, okay?

676
00:30:37,618 --> 00:30:38,619
Okay.

677
00:30:46,583 --> 00:30:47,758
San Francisco
kind of sucked.

678
00:30:47,846 --> 00:30:49,325
Mm-hmm.

679
00:30:49,412 --> 00:30:51,675
I asked Sarah to help
prep my testimony.

680
00:30:51,762 --> 00:30:54,635
It turned into a fight.

681
00:30:54,722 --> 00:30:57,812
Iturned it into a fight.
She brought up my dad.

682
00:30:57,899 --> 00:30:59,466
Right.

683
00:30:59,553 --> 00:31:01,337
She just goes straight
for the jugular.

684
00:31:01,424 --> 00:31:02,643
She's tough, you know?

685
00:31:02,730 --> 00:31:04,340
She doesn't
look it, but..

686
00:31:04,427 --> 00:31:07,082
When she brings it,
she's as tough as any of us.

687
00:31:07,169 --> 00:31:10,912
You should see
her in court.

688
00:31:10,999 --> 00:31:15,221
She's tearing up a witness,
just really goes to town on 'em.

689
00:31:15,308 --> 00:31:16,613
It's pretty awesome.

690
00:31:18,093 --> 00:31:20,269
'She was doing exactly
what I asked her do.'

691
00:31:21,749 --> 00:31:24,970
I got mad 'cause
she was doing it so well.

692
00:31:25,057 --> 00:31:26,449
I'm such an idiot.

693
00:31:26,536 --> 00:31:28,060
Thanks for listening.

694
00:31:30,105 --> 00:31:32,325
[snoring]

695
00:31:34,675 --> 00:31:37,765
Mr. Green. Nice of you
to show up. You're under arrest.

696
00:31:37,852 --> 00:31:39,549
This is harassment.

697
00:31:39,636 --> 00:31:42,639
No, it's a raid. Your friend,
Martin, turned over a new leaf.

698
00:31:42,726 --> 00:31:44,815
You were under it.
Ron, cuff him.

699
00:31:44,903 --> 00:31:46,339
Hey, boss.
We arresting the bartender?

700
00:31:46,426 --> 00:31:48,167
Why not?
The more, the merrier.

701
00:31:54,347 --> 00:31:55,914
[door opening]

702
00:31:56,915 --> 00:31:58,960
Coast is clear.
Come on.

703
00:31:59,047 --> 00:32:00,092
Where are we heading?

704
00:32:00,179 --> 00:32:01,484
To solve the case.

705
00:32:01,571 --> 00:32:03,225
Didn't we just do that?

706
00:32:05,140 --> 00:32:07,316
Come on, man.
I'm getting so tired of this.

707
00:32:07,403 --> 00:32:09,362
I found out
my relationship is a sham

708
00:32:09,449 --> 00:32:11,190
all my friends
think I'm a punk.

709
00:32:11,277 --> 00:32:14,236
Now you got somebody else who
wants to dump on Doc Dugan.

710
00:32:15,455 --> 00:32:17,326
Truth hurts, I know.

711
00:32:17,413 --> 00:32:18,893
It's biologically-based,
actually.

712
00:32:18,980 --> 00:32:21,200
Our brains process sound
faster than they do light

713
00:32:21,287 --> 00:32:22,766
but light moves
faster than sound.

714
00:32:22,853 --> 00:32:24,551
So our brains are
constantly shifting reality

715
00:32:24,638 --> 00:32:25,813
so the world syncs up.

716
00:32:25,900 --> 00:32:27,249
So?

717
00:32:27,336 --> 00:32:29,556
Only when someone is
standing 30 yards away

718
00:32:29,643 --> 00:32:32,341
do we see and hear the world
exactly as it is.

719
00:32:32,428 --> 00:32:34,126
That's when your brain,
sound, and light

720
00:32:34,213 --> 00:32:35,692
are all in perfect harmony.

721
00:32:35,779 --> 00:32:38,782
The rest of the time,
we're living in a world of lies.

722
00:32:38,869 --> 00:32:41,002
Lies are what make
the world make sense.

723
00:32:41,089 --> 00:32:42,961
Ergo, the truth hurts.

724
00:32:51,230 --> 00:32:54,363
No, no, no, not Anna.
No, no. Nah-nah. No way.

725
00:32:54,450 --> 00:32:56,191
- Have a seat.
- 'Not again.'

726
00:32:58,759 --> 00:32:59,890
I can't.

727
00:32:59,978 --> 00:33:01,892
I know, but sit.

728
00:33:04,199 --> 00:33:06,462
My name is Patrick Jane.
I saw your interview

729
00:33:06,549 --> 00:33:08,029
with Agent Lisbon.
Interesting stuff.

730
00:33:08,116 --> 00:33:09,378
[Anna]
'Is there a problem?'

731
00:33:09,465 --> 00:33:11,293
Yes, there is.

732
00:33:11,380 --> 00:33:13,339
The problem is
you were telling a lie.

733
00:33:13,426 --> 00:33:15,428
Is this about my alibi?
'cause I can--

734
00:33:15,515 --> 00:33:16,995
Nothing to do
with your alibi.

735
00:33:17,082 --> 00:33:19,258
You said you hated
your ex-husband

736
00:33:19,345 --> 00:33:22,913
'but clearly that's
not the case.'

737
00:33:23,001 --> 00:33:26,613
Hey. Here's the file
on the mob bomber, Louie Boom.

738
00:33:26,700 --> 00:33:29,442
Lisbon says give it a read
before we bring him in.

739
00:33:29,529 --> 00:33:31,922
Alex, would you
come with me, please?

740
00:33:32,010 --> 00:33:33,707
[door opening]

741
00:33:33,794 --> 00:33:35,491
We need to go over
your statement.

742
00:33:35,578 --> 00:33:37,450
Oh, yeah, sure.

743
00:33:40,192 --> 00:33:41,671
You don't even know me.

744
00:33:41,758 --> 00:33:43,978
I could see through
half an inch of glass.

745
00:33:44,065 --> 00:33:46,850
You're deeply in love
with your dead ex-husband.

746
00:33:46,937 --> 00:33:48,809
What business is it
of yours?

747
00:33:48,896 --> 00:33:50,854
Just tell me the truth.

748
00:33:50,941 --> 00:33:51,942
Just say the truth.

749
00:33:52,030 --> 00:33:55,120
Fine. You're right.

750
00:33:56,512 --> 00:33:59,080
I loved him.

751
00:33:59,167 --> 00:34:03,476
Somewhere deep down in him was
the good man I used to know.

752
00:34:03,563 --> 00:34:06,218
It got lost when
he retired, but..

753
00:34:06,305 --> 00:34:09,264
he would have found
his way back.

754
00:34:09,351 --> 00:34:10,657
I know he would have.

755
00:34:12,137 --> 00:34:13,964
And now I'll never
see him again

756
00:34:14,052 --> 00:34:16,054
'and I'll never
get to tell him.'

757
00:34:19,405 --> 00:34:20,580
Okay.

758
00:34:22,103 --> 00:34:23,800
Well, that should do it.

759
00:34:25,802 --> 00:34:26,977
Hey, wait.

760
00:34:29,415 --> 00:34:31,112
- Leave her alone.
- Okay.

761
00:34:31,199 --> 00:34:33,375
- Trevor?
- Annie.

762
00:34:33,462 --> 00:34:35,464
What, what are you..

763
00:34:35,551 --> 00:34:38,032
- They said, I thought..
- A trick.

764
00:34:38,119 --> 00:34:40,382
To catch the killer.
I just blew it.

765
00:34:40,469 --> 00:34:41,557
What?

766
00:34:41,644 --> 00:34:44,299
I'm sorry.

767
00:34:44,386 --> 00:34:46,954
I'm so sorry
about everything.

768
00:34:47,041 --> 00:34:48,086
[groaning]

769
00:34:53,961 --> 00:34:55,397
The guys from vespers
are downstairs.

770
00:34:55,484 --> 00:34:56,964
- Did he go for it?
- Yep.

771
00:34:57,051 --> 00:35:00,620
- Did he go for what?
- Sir.

772
00:35:00,707 --> 00:35:02,230
[indistinct chattering]

773
00:35:02,317 --> 00:35:04,928
Doc Dugan is still alive?

774
00:35:05,015 --> 00:35:06,408
Yes, sir.

775
00:35:06,495 --> 00:35:10,586
'It was his assistant,
Jake Stewart, that died.'

776
00:35:10,673 --> 00:35:12,893
And you chose to hide
this fact from me.

777
00:35:12,980 --> 00:35:15,678
From everyone, for the sake
of the investigation.

778
00:35:18,507 --> 00:35:20,988
- A trick.
- I wouldn't call it a trick.

779
00:35:21,075 --> 00:35:24,513
- Did this work?
- It's still in progress.

780
00:35:24,600 --> 00:35:26,602
Okay, well, um..

781
00:35:29,562 --> 00:35:31,477
Let's hope it turns out
positively, huh?

782
00:35:31,564 --> 00:35:32,652
Yes, sir.

783
00:35:35,437 --> 00:35:38,397
Do you think he would mind
if I got an autograph?

784
00:35:38,484 --> 00:35:39,659
Just a..

785
00:35:42,357 --> 00:35:46,187
Yeah. I'll just..
You know, give them a second.

786
00:35:47,406 --> 00:35:48,450
Good idea, sir.

787
00:36:02,899 --> 00:36:04,553
Any one of these guys

788
00:36:04,640 --> 00:36:07,513
looks like the man you saw
running from the explosion?

789
00:36:07,600 --> 00:36:08,644
You mean Louie Boom.

790
00:36:08,731 --> 00:36:10,907
I mean the man you saw.

791
00:36:10,994 --> 00:36:12,996
I can' tell you
which one is Louie.

792
00:36:13,083 --> 00:36:14,650
It'll taint the lineup.

793
00:36:14,737 --> 00:36:16,609
That's him. Number four.

794
00:36:20,308 --> 00:36:22,397
That's the guy
from the parking lot.

795
00:36:22,484 --> 00:36:24,617
You're sure?
That's Louie?

796
00:36:24,704 --> 00:36:25,879
That's the guy.

797
00:36:25,966 --> 00:36:29,622
Rigsby, come on in.

798
00:36:33,626 --> 00:36:35,976
- Hey, guys.
- He saw me.

799
00:36:36,063 --> 00:36:37,847
- Can he do that?
- We're all friends.

800
00:36:37,934 --> 00:36:39,240
What's going on here?

801
00:36:39,327 --> 00:36:41,677
I'm special Agent Rigsby.

802
00:36:41,764 --> 00:36:44,202
Oh, then, I must have
made a mistake.

803
00:36:44,289 --> 00:36:45,725
You wanted to ace
the test

804
00:36:45,812 --> 00:36:48,554
so you took a little peek.
I made the test.

805
00:36:48,641 --> 00:36:51,339
I made the whole thing up.
There is no "Louie Boom."

806
00:36:51,426 --> 00:36:54,516
[Teresa]
'We gave you a chance
to look, and you took it.'

807
00:37:00,914 --> 00:37:02,394
You said Martin talked.

808
00:37:02,481 --> 00:37:05,266
He said there was no way
Green was behind Doc's death.

809
00:37:05,353 --> 00:37:07,137
Lot of people
didn't like Doc

810
00:37:07,225 --> 00:37:09,923
but most of them made
more money if Doc was alive.

811
00:37:10,010 --> 00:37:12,578
Only Steve here made
more money with Doc dead.

812
00:37:12,665 --> 00:37:15,755
You have a house filled
with memorabilia signed by Doc.

813
00:37:15,842 --> 00:37:17,887
He dies, the prices
go through the roof.

814
00:37:17,974 --> 00:37:19,106
You make a fortune.

815
00:37:19,193 --> 00:37:20,455
Come on.

816
00:37:20,542 --> 00:37:22,152
I saw somebody running away.

817
00:37:22,240 --> 00:37:24,372
- It was an explosion.
- People run from explosions.

818
00:37:24,459 --> 00:37:26,418
Your prints on that file
should be enough

819
00:37:26,505 --> 00:37:28,681
to get a search warrant.
Let's have a look.

820
00:37:28,768 --> 00:37:30,422
The truth
shall set us free.

821
00:37:30,509 --> 00:37:32,554
Well, maybe not all of us.

822
00:37:47,917 --> 00:37:49,179
Where am I going?

823
00:37:49,267 --> 00:37:50,964
Booking, for the murder
of Jacob Stewart

824
00:37:51,051 --> 00:37:52,748
and the attempted murder
of Trevor Dugan.

825
00:37:52,835 --> 00:37:54,794
We found bomb residue
in your garage

826
00:37:54,881 --> 00:37:56,404
and research
in your web history.

827
00:37:56,491 --> 00:37:59,451
Add that to the fact that you
tried to frame Louie Boom.

828
00:37:59,538 --> 00:38:01,975
Hey!

829
00:38:02,062 --> 00:38:04,238
There's a roomful of it
back at your place.

830
00:38:04,325 --> 00:38:05,500
'Stuff signed by me'

831
00:38:05,587 --> 00:38:09,461
packaged to sell
after I'm dead.

832
00:38:09,548 --> 00:38:10,940
I thought we were
friends, Steve.

833
00:38:11,027 --> 00:38:12,246
Friends?

834
00:38:12,333 --> 00:38:13,813
Friends 'cause I get
your coffee?

835
00:38:13,900 --> 00:38:16,598
Because I cover up
every mess you make?

836
00:38:16,685 --> 00:38:18,992
Hide your girlfriend
from your wife?

837
00:38:19,079 --> 00:38:21,255
What'd you ever do for me?

838
00:38:21,342 --> 00:38:23,692
Gave you a job, man.

839
00:38:23,779 --> 00:38:25,999
I was the number one
draft pick.

840
00:38:26,086 --> 00:38:28,175
It should be me
wearing that damn ring.

841
00:38:28,262 --> 00:38:29,872
Youshould beworking for me!

842
00:38:29,959 --> 00:38:32,919
So you built a bomb and you
stuck it on Doug's car.

843
00:38:33,006 --> 00:38:35,051
I just wanted what
was coming to me.

844
00:38:35,138 --> 00:38:39,229
I never meant for Jake to die.
He was a good kid.

845
00:38:39,317 --> 00:38:40,970
Alright, that's enough.
Take him away.

846
00:38:42,755 --> 00:38:44,147
Let's go.

847
00:38:55,942 --> 00:38:58,205
Let me get that.

848
00:38:58,292 --> 00:38:59,598
[door closing]

849
00:38:59,685 --> 00:39:01,687
Wanna take a seat?

850
00:39:04,646 --> 00:39:06,692
Look, I, uh..

851
00:39:06,779 --> 00:39:08,737
I wanted to say I'm sorry
about yesterday.

852
00:39:08,824 --> 00:39:11,784
It's just.. Well, you called me
a dirty cop, Sarah.

853
00:39:11,871 --> 00:39:13,916
Oh, God, I would
never call you that.

854
00:39:14,003 --> 00:39:16,397
I was trying to prepare
you for the defense lawyer.

855
00:39:16,484 --> 00:39:18,573
Yeah, I know.
Thank you.

856
00:39:18,660 --> 00:39:20,619
The trial was
a breeze compared to you.

857
00:39:20,706 --> 00:39:23,012
- Oh, you did good?
- Yeah.

858
00:39:23,099 --> 00:39:25,537
I know you can't help it.
You're a defense attorney.

859
00:39:25,624 --> 00:39:27,103
What's that supposed
to mean?

860
00:39:27,190 --> 00:39:31,499
Oh, come on. You guys
protect criminals for money.

861
00:39:31,586 --> 00:39:33,327
Is that what you
think we do?

862
00:39:33,414 --> 00:39:35,808
Tell me, what is it
exactly that cops do?

863
00:39:35,895 --> 00:39:38,898
We put the bad guys away
if we can get around you.

864
00:39:38,985 --> 00:39:41,466
Sometimes you put
away good guys.

865
00:39:41,553 --> 00:39:42,815
- Oh, yeah?
- Yeah.

866
00:39:42,902 --> 00:39:44,860
We have to deal
with cops every day

867
00:39:44,947 --> 00:39:46,340
who can't even
spell "Miranda."

868
00:39:46,427 --> 00:39:48,777
I'm gonna do whatever
I can for my client.

869
00:39:48,864 --> 00:39:50,823
Now do you have
a problem with that?

870
00:39:50,910 --> 00:39:54,609
No.
No problem at all.

871
00:39:54,696 --> 00:39:55,654
Mmm.

872
00:39:55,741 --> 00:39:58,396
[both moaning]

873
00:40:03,226 --> 00:40:07,796
We got him, Mrs. Stewart,
thanks to your help.

874
00:40:07,883 --> 00:40:10,799
No, you're the one
that deserves the thanks.

875
00:40:10,886 --> 00:40:12,235
Doc, too.

876
00:40:12,322 --> 00:40:13,889
'He was really useful.'

877
00:40:13,976 --> 00:40:17,589
I'd like to pay
for Jake's funeral if I can.

878
00:40:17,676 --> 00:40:19,199
Anything else you need.

879
00:40:19,286 --> 00:40:21,723
Thank you, but I don't need
handouts, Mr. Dugan.

880
00:40:21,810 --> 00:40:25,640
Call me Trevor, please,
and it's not a handout.

881
00:40:25,727 --> 00:40:26,989
It's what I owe Jake.

882
00:40:28,600 --> 00:40:31,211
Alright, then.

883
00:40:31,298 --> 00:40:33,431
Thank you. Thank you.

884
00:40:37,217 --> 00:40:40,481
I'd like to stay
a little longer if I can.

885
00:40:40,568 --> 00:40:42,004
Sure.

886
00:40:42,091 --> 00:40:44,529
Again, I am so sorry
for your loss.

887
00:40:47,314 --> 00:40:48,489
[door opening]

888
00:40:48,576 --> 00:40:50,752
I was hoping we could

889
00:40:50,839 --> 00:40:53,712
talk a little bit more
about Jake.

890
00:40:53,799 --> 00:40:55,148
Sure.

891
00:40:55,235 --> 00:40:57,672
I hear he was one hell
of a ballplayer himself.

892
00:40:57,759 --> 00:41:00,806
Yeah, he won the state
championship.

893
00:41:00,893 --> 00:41:04,113
- Senior year, yeah.
- No.

894
00:41:04,200 --> 00:41:05,158
Hey.

895
00:41:05,245 --> 00:41:06,376
All good?

896
00:41:06,464 --> 00:41:09,379
Yeah. Doc seems like
a changed man.

897
00:41:09,467 --> 00:41:11,643
He's had a perspective
few of us ever get.

898
00:41:11,730 --> 00:41:13,645
Energizing, I expect.

899
00:41:13,732 --> 00:41:15,168
Would you want that?

900
00:41:15,255 --> 00:41:17,736
To hear what people say
about you when you're dead?

901
00:41:17,823 --> 00:41:20,086
When I'm dead, they can
burn me in effigy

902
00:41:20,173 --> 00:41:23,263
or build a statue or forget
about me completely.

903
00:41:23,350 --> 00:41:25,308
- I couldn't care less.
- You don't mean that.

904
00:41:25,395 --> 00:41:26,919
Everybody wants
to be remembered.

905
00:41:27,006 --> 00:41:29,835
- Childish vanity.
- I want to be remembered.

906
00:41:29,922 --> 00:41:32,359
You shall be, fondly,
but you won't care about it

907
00:41:32,446 --> 00:41:33,882
because when you're dead,
you're dead,

908
00:41:33,969 --> 00:41:36,102
and until then
there's ice cream.

909
00:41:36,189 --> 00:41:38,452
[silverware clattering]

910
00:41:46,982 --> 00:41:48,593
Mmmm.

